From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: a process's own display update is not taken for another's

Since 1120 a process takes the monitors' serial as read only when no other
process changed the monitors while it read them. But a process that writes
the display devices itself (lock_display_devices with force: one that
found no display configuration it could read) moves the serial on by
writing, and then took its own write for another process's change. A
process with no display driver of its own -- start.exe, a console program,
on a desktop it did not make -- so wrote its placeholder monitor (0x0, the
null driver's) again on every call, over the desktop's real ones: the
log filled with "Failed to read display config", and other programs'
windows got no driver at all ("Application tried to create a window, but
no driver could be loaded"). The cloak, display and desktop gates went red
with 1120 (2026-10-06), and in a session programs could start without
windows.

The serial is now read again after the process's own write, so only
another process's change counts as one.

Test: test/cloak-gate.sh (mutant SG_MUTANT_OWN_COMMIT_STALE in
sysparams.c fails it); test/dpifollow-gate.sh still passes, and still
catches 1120's SG_MUTANT_WORKAREA_STALE_SERIAL.
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -2385,6 +2385,16 @@
         if (!(status = update_display_devices( &ctx ))) commit_display_devices( &ctx );
         else WARN( "Failed to update display devices, status %#x\n", status );
         release_display_manager_ctx( &ctx );
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_OWN_COMMIT_STALE
+        /* Stained Glass OS: the devices this process just wrote moved the
+         * monitors' serial on: its own change, not another process's made
+         * while it read them (1120's check in
+         * update_display_cache_from_registry). Taken as another's, a process
+         * with no display driver of its own wrote its placeholder monitor
+         * again on every call, over the desktop's real ones, and other
+         * processes' windows got no driver */
+        serial = get_monitor_update_serial();
+#endif
 
         ret = update_display_cache_from_registry( serial );
     }
